    "bodyHtml": "<div class=\"cd-shot\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"assets/img/044.jpg\" alt=\"General Authority for Minors' Affairs — staff intranet homepage: quick links, media center, news, events, announcements and shared assets\" width=\"1150\" height=\"1615\" loading=\"eager\" fetchpriority=\"high\"></div>\n        <p class=\"cd-caption\">The staff homepage works as a dashboard, not a landing page — Quick Links, Media Center,\n          Latest News, Upcoming Events, Announcements and Shared Assets each get their own card, so an employee can scan\n          every department update in a single screen instead of digging through a menu.</p>\n        <div class=\"cd-pair\">\n          <div class=\"cd-shot\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"assets/img/045.jpg\" alt=\"General Authority for Minors' Affairs — internal Medical Services catalogue, searchable grid of benefit cards\" width=\"600\" height=\"886\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n          <div class=\"cd-shot\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"assets/img/046.jpg\" alt=\"General Authority for Minors' Affairs — individual service detail page, reached from the catalogue\" width=\"600\" height=\"626\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n        </div>\n        <p class=\"cd-caption\">Left — the internal benefits/services catalogue, searchable and browsable as a card\n          grid. Right — a single service opened from that grid, following the same hero-plus-detail pattern used\n          across every module so staff never have to learn a new layout twice.</p>\n        <div class=\"cd-pair\">\n          <div class=\"cd-shot\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"assets/img/047.jpg\" alt=\"General Authority for Minors' Affairs — Employee Directory with name search, department filter and paginated table\" width=\"600\" height=\"681\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n          <div class=\"cd-shot\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"assets/img/048.jpg\" alt=\"General Authority for Minors' Affairs — Department Workspace: document library, projects list, task tracker, delegation and leave calendar\" width=\"600\" height=\"1768\" loading=\"lazy\"></div>\n        </div>\n        <p class=\"cd-caption\">Left — the Employee Directory, pairing a name search with a department filter over\n          a paginated table built for a real headcount. Right — a Department Workspace stacking five data-heavy\n          modules — Document Library, Projects List, Task Tracker, Delegation and Leave Calendar — on one\n          reusable grid/table component with a consistent action set (New, Upload, Edit in grid view, Export to Excel, Share).</p>\n        <div class=\"cd-copy\">\n          <h4 class=\"lbl\">The Brief</h4>\n          <p>The General Authority for Minors' Affairs is an independent Qatari government entity charged with the\n            comprehensive care of minors under its guardianship — managing their funds, property and inheritance,\n            and representing their legal and financial interests until they come of age. Behind that public mandate sits\n            a large in-house team running case management, HR and cross-department coordination, and the brief was to\n            give that team a single digital workplace: a homepage that surfaces news, events and shared resources at a\n            glance, a searchable employee directory, department-level workspaces for document control and project\n            tracking, and an internal benefits catalogue — replacing what would otherwise be a scatter of email\n            threads, shared drives and paper requests. My scope covered the full UI and UX end-to-end across the whole system.</p>\n          <h4 class=\"lbl\">My Approach</h4>\n          <p>The homepage is organised as a set of independent cards rather than one long feed, so six very different\n            content types — links, media, news, events, announcements, shared files — can sit on one screen\n            without competing for attention. Every data-heavy module in the Department Workspace (documents, projects,\n            tasks, delegation, leave) reuses the same grid/table component and the same action bar, so the system reads\n            as one coherent product instead of five different tools bolted together, and status is always shown as a\n            coloured pill — Completed, In Progress, Not Started — so a manager can read the state of a queue\n            at a glance rather than opening each record.</p>\n          <h4 class=\"lbl\">Outcome</h4>\n          <p>A single bilingual internal portal that gives staff one place for company news, a searchable employee\n            directory, department-level document and project management, delegation and leave tracking, and internal\n            service requests — for an authority whose own public mandate is protecting some of Qatar's most\n            vulnerable citizens.</p>\n          \n        </div>"
