    "bodyHtml": "<div class=\"cd-shot\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"assets/img/058.jpg\" alt=\"Qatar University — assistant widget\" width=\"1100\" height=\"802\" loading=\"eager\" fetchpriority=\"high\"></div>\n        <div class=\"cd-copy\">\n          <h4 class=\"lbl\">The Brief</h4>\n        <p>As Qatar's oldest and largest national university, QU's site has to serve prospective students, enrolled students, faculty, researchers and the public at once — spanning admissions, a dozen colleges, research output and campus life. That breadth is an information-architecture problem before it's a visual one, doubled by a full Arabic/English mirror of every path. My part of that scope was the Results microsite — the page students and staff rely on to look up and manage examination results — including every form and lookup step inside it.</p>\n        <h4 class=\"lbl\">My Approach</h4>\n        <p>I approached it as a lookup-first flow rather than a blank form: a student or staff member enters their record identifier once, and the page resolves it into a clear, structured results view instead of a set of fields to fill in without knowing what's ahead. Every label carries a bilingual Arabic/English mirror, and the forms inside the microsite follow QU's existing navigation and visual language so the Results section never feels like a detour from the rest of the university's site.</p>\n        <h4 class=\"lbl\">Outcome</h4>\n        <p>The Results microsite shipped as part of QU's live site, giving students and staff a dedicated, self-service way to look up and manage academic results without a manual, in-person process.</p>\n          \n        </div>"
